



1. GIT CHECKOUT

git checkout command is used to move between branches
or to check/view a previous version of files.

Example:

git checkout branch-name

Example:

git checkout main





-------------------------
2. WORKING DIRECTORY
-------------------------

The Working Directory is the place where we create,
edit, modify, and delete our project files.

Example:

file.txt → edited

Any changes made to files are first present in the
Working Directory.

To check the current status of files:

git status





-------------------------
3. STAGING AREA
-------------------------

The Staging Area is the intermediate area between the
Working Directory and the Local Repository.

We use the Staging Area to select which changes we want
to save in the next commit.

To move changes from Working Directory to Staging Area:

git add file.txt

To add all files:

git add .


FLOW:

Working Directory
        |
        | git add
        ↓
Staging Area





-------------------------
4. LOCAL REPOSITORY
-------------------------

The Local Repository stores the commits that we create.

After adding changes to the Staging Area, we save them
permanently by creating a commit.

Command:

git commit -m "message"


FLOW:

Working Directory
        |
        | git add
        ↓
Staging Area
        |
        | git commit
        ↓
Local Repository


GIT RESET :


git reset command is used to undo changes or move back
to a previous commit.

There are mainly 3 types of git reset:

1. git reset --soft
2. git reset --mixed
3. git reset --hard





1. GIT RESET --SOFT

Command:

git reset --soft HEAD~1

This removes the previous/latest commit.

But:

✓ Changes remain in the Staging Area
✓ Changes remain in the Working Directory

It only moves the commit backward.

Example:

Before:

Working Directory → Staging Area → Local Repository
                                      Commit


After git reset --soft HEAD~1:

Working Directory → Staging Area
       ✓                 ✓

The commit is removed, but changes are still staged.





2. GIT RESET --MIXED

Command:

git reset --mixed HEAD~1

OR:

git reset HEAD~1

(--mixed is the default mode)

This removes the latest commit and also removes the
changes from the Staging Area.

But:

✓ Changes remain in the Working Directory
✗ Changes are removed from Staging Area
✗ Commit is removed


Example:

Before:

Working Directory → Staging Area → Local Repository


After git reset HEAD~1:

Working Directory
       ✓

Staging Area
       ✗

Commit
       ✗





3. GIT RESET --HARD

Command:

git reset --hard HEAD~1

This removes everything after the selected commit.

✗ Commit is removed
✗ Staging Area changes are removed
✗ Working Directory changes are removed


WARNING:

git reset --hard can delete changes permanently.





RESET COMPARISON



git reset --soft

Commit          → Removed
Staging Area    → Changes remain
Working Files   → Changes remain


git reset --mixed

Commit          → Removed
Staging Area    → Changes removed
Working Files   → Changes remain


git reset --hard

Commit          → Removed
Staging Area    → Changes removed
Working Files   → Changes removed


IMPORTANT FLOW



        WORKING DIRECTORY
               |
               | git add
               ↓
          STAGING AREA
               |
               | git commit
               ↓
        LOCAL REPOSITORY


====================================================
              QUICK REVISION
====================================================

git status
→ Shows the status of files


git add file.txt
→ Adds a file to the Staging Area


git add .
→ Adds all files to the Staging Area


git commit -m "message"
→ Saves staged changes as a commit


git checkout branch-name
→ Switches to another branch


git reset --soft HEAD~1
→ Undo commit, keep staged changes


git reset HEAD~1
→ Undo commit, keep file changes


git reset --hard HEAD~1
→ Undo everythhing